{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"value object value object","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md","fileName":"Value Object.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Value Object","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-22 20:53\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Domain Model Building Blocks]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Domain Model Building Blocks","Ubiquitous Language","Domain Entity","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"value object value object","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md","fileName":"Value Object.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Value Object","heading":"Value Object","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A value object represents a domain concept defined by its values rather than by a continuing identity. It gives primitive data an explicit type in the [[Ubiquitous Language]], controls how valid instances are created, and can define meaningful operations over those values. A price, title, or user identifier can reject invalid input before it reaches a [[Domain Entity]]. Value objects are preferably immutable, so replacing one value does not create hidden changes elsewhere.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Domain Model Building Blocks","Ubiquitous Language","Domain Entity","hands-ondomain-drivendesignwithnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"value object value object","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Value Object.md